Formats
description: the open format keyword - a named constraint on the abstract value, never a decoder
- >
  `format` is open: a MIME type (`image/png`, `text/markdown`), a value format kept from JSON
  Schema (`date-time`, `email`, `uri`), or a yamlover-defined view (`x-yamlover-chapter`) - a
  named **constraint** on the abstract value, driving how it is rendered and edited. A format
  refines a facet; it is not a type ([terminology](*::language:logical-graph:terminology)), and since the
  concrete/format split it **never selects decoding**: how bytes or text become the value is the
  [concrete](*::language:concretes)'s job (`concrete: yamlover/stream`, `binary/int32/le`,
  `base64`, `text/utf-8`).
- >
  Against JSON Schema: `format` replaces `contentMediaType` (what the content *is*), and
  `concrete` replaces `contentEncoding` (how it is *carried*). The pre-split spellings - a codec
  or a sub-document encoding stated as `format:` (`format: int32/le`, `format: yamlover/meta`) -
  read forever but are not authored anymore. The known formats are catalogued in the
  [logical graph's format chapter](*::language:logical-graph:format); resolution order is the
  [meta charter](*::meta)'s subject.
